    "topic_title": "Inverse Trigonometric Functions",
    "stem": "What is the exact value of arccos(cos(5π/3))?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "5π/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unrestricted cancellation error: arccos(cos θ) does not always equal θ. Although 5π/3 has the required cosine, it lies outside the inverse cosine range [0, π]. Fix: Check whether the original angle lies in [0, π] before canceling inverse cosine and cosine."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "−π/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Wrong principal range error: −π/3 has the required cosine, but inverse cosine cannot return a negative angle. Its output must lie in [0, π]. Fix: Find an angle with the same cosine inside [0, π]."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "2π/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Quadrant sign error: cos(2π/3) = −1/2, but cos(5π/3) = 1/2. Being inside the inverse cosine range is necessary, but the cosine value must also match. Fix: Check the cosine’s sign in each angle’s quadrant before selecting the inverse cosine value."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "π/3",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Since cos(5π/3) = 1/2, the expression equals arccos(1/2). Inverse cosine returns an angle in [0, π], and π/3 is the angle in that range whose cosine is 1/2."
     }
    ]
   },
